WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:07.389
In an age of revisionist fairy tales like
Frozen, Maleficent and Into The Woods, Disney

00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:14.309
stay relatively old-school in the live-action
remake of their classic 1950s animation, Cinderella.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:19.240
Downton Abbey star Lily James brings a genuine
sweetness and lightness to the role of the

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.240
good-hearted Ella whose stepfamily turns her
into a skivvy, nicknaming her Cinderella.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.590
Richard Madden, who's best known as Robb Stark
in Game Of Thrones, gives a charming performance

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.480
as the Prince.
And James and Madden make a perfect on-screen

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:34.950
pair.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.510
As Cinders’ stepmother, Lady Tremaine, Cate
Blanchett shows, yet again, what an exceptional

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.570
actress she is.
You won’t be able to tear your eyes away

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.910
from her whenever she’s on screen, whether
she’s doling out chores and making catty

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:54.320
comments to Cinders, scheming her way up the
social ladder, rolling her eyes at her talentless

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.730
offspring, or draping herself over furniture
and in doorways.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.840
Think Bette Davis meets Joan Crawford. In
short, Blanchett is sensational.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.579
Holliday Grainger from The Borgias and Sophie
McShera from Downton Abbey also do a great

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:12.819
job as Cinderella’s spiteful and selfish
stepsisters.

00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:18.340
And the always brilliant Helena Bonham Carter
swoops in as Cinders’ Fairy Godmother and

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.469
almost steals the show, despite having just
one scene.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:25.939
In fact, she’s so fantastic that she leaves
you wishing she had more screen-time.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:31.219
Still, we do hear her voice throughout as
she narrates various parts of the film, and,

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:36.490
in a nice touch over the end credits, she
sings Bibbidi Bobbidi Boo from Disney’s

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:37.759
animated Cinderella!

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.149
Speaking of music, there’s a lovely score
by Patrick Doyle, who’s worked many times

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:46.469
before with Cinderella’s director Kenneth
Branagh and whose music you’ll have heard

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:51.249
on other films such as Pixar’s Brave.
Except for the folk song Lavender’s Blue,

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:55.829
there aren’t any songs in the film and,
to be honest, I did miss that, especially

00:01:55.829 --> 00:02:01.359
given the classic songs we know from Disney’s
animated features and the superb live-action

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:02.729
feature Enchanted.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:07.200
If you’re wondering what the movie’s transformation
scenes are like, let me tell you that they

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.629
are just spectacular – a dazzling, dreamy
delight.

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:15.579
There are two transformations you’ll be
expecting, the ones before and after Cinders

00:02:15.579 --> 00:02:20.239
goes to the Ball, AND there’s a little extra
surprise to look forward to.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:24.010
That’s all I’m going to say about that,
as I don’t want to spoil it for you!

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.459
Oh, and just as you’d expect, the scenes
where Cinders arrives at the palace and dances

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.390
with the Prince are brimming with old-school
style.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.420
Costume designer Sandy Powell has done an
incredible job, creating the most staggeringly

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.490
stunning costumes.
Essentially, she’s a shoo-in for the Oscars

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.560
&amp; BAFTAs next year.
From the gorgeous 1940s-inspired gowns Cate

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.159
Blanchett wears, to Cinders’ servant girl
costumes and her beautiful ball gown, to Helena

00:02:51.159 --> 00:02:56.680
Bonham Carter’s fairy godmother get-up,
to the stepsisters’ deliberately gaudy matching

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:02.689
garb, the costume work is faultless.
And I just loved the wonderfully garish ball

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:07.469
gowns worn by the tasteless stepsisters – they
looked like they’d eaten several tons of

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:11.049
Quality Streets chocolates and then dressed
themselves in the wrappers!

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:16.670
As for the worlds of Cinderella and the Prince,
they’re beautifully detailed and rich, thanks

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:21.310
to production designer Dante Ferretti.
And cinematographer Haris Zambarloukos, whose

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:25.499
work you’ll have seen in Thor and Mamma
Mia!, gives the film a magical sheen.

00:03:25.499 --> 00:03:29.920
British director Kenneth Branagh’s take
on the Cinderella story is an unashamedly

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.659
sweet slice of cinema which focuses on the
three c’s: courage, kindness and oodles

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:39.099
of colour.
Given the hyper-cynical era we live in, it’s

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:44.560
kind of a bold move for Branagh to shine a
nostalgic light on a character of such unalloyed

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.920
goodness as Cinderella.
As for the look of the film, there are lots

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.780
of lavish-looking crane shots that sweep up
and over parts of the palace as well as the

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.349
landscape.
They do look great but start to feel a touch

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.480
overused as the film progresses.
Oh, and if you’ve watched many Branagh films

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.279
before, you’ll know that he loves a good
circle shot, where he swirls around his actors,

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.650
and Cinderella is no exception as you’ll
see when Cinders and the Prince first meet.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.920
And it really does give their meet-cute a
kind of appropriately dizzying quality.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.430
Screenwriter Chris Weitz mainly sticks to
the traditional tale we all know, making some

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.270
nice nods to the original animation throughout.
He does throw in a few noteworthy changes,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.729
too, including an early meet-cute between
Cinderella and the Prince, which makes it

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:34.729
feel as though the pair are on a more equal
footing, despite the difference in their status.

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:40.240
Weitz also adds some nuance to Cate Blanchett’s
wicked stepmother, and he answers our potential

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.710
questions about why Cinders doesn’t just
run away from her horrible stepfamily and

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.590
why they don’t recognise her at the Ball.
While I’m not a huge fan of the movie’s

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.900
opening scenes, which take us through Cinderella’s
childhood and last a little too long, they

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:58.199
do fit with the dreamy, fantastical, magical
feel of the whole film.

00:04:58.199 --> 00:05:03.250
So, to sum up: Cinderella is an incredibly
colourful and charming confection that brings

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:09.000
to life Disney’s classic animation by tipping
its beautifully tailored hat to nostalgia.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.250
Cinderella gets 4 out of 5 Flicks.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:16.270
